then:

\xrightarrow{\text{Ouroboros}}
\text{learned delta}

then:

\xrightarrow{\text{Accordion}}
\boxed{\text{tiny reconstructable Seal}}

That’s an instrument pipeline.

And I see another abstraction now:

Interfaces are artificial senses

A microscope makes the microscopic actionable.

A telescope makes distant light actionable.

A graph makes network structure actionable.

Calculus makes continuous change manipulable.

Code makes procedures executable.

A model makes intuition transmissible.

So:

\boxed{
\text{Interface}
=
\text{transformation that makes previously inaccessible structure actionable}
}

That’s stronger than “communication boundary.”

And therefore the PatternDex should hunt for:

\boxed{
\text{Structure exists}
+
\text{we can't manipulate it well}
=
\text{interface opportunity}
}

That gives us a systematic Interface Forge:

Find inaccessible relation → choose new sensory/representational surface → preserve relevant invariants → make operations possible → test → simplify → Seal.

We’re no longer just collecting patterns.

We’re building new organs for reasoning.